Actually, it's a (quite) well-known fact that tactics is "easier" than  
strategy in the sense that it is currently almost impossible to beat a  
computer on tactics while it is almost trivial to win on strategy...
Consider these :
Computers can play chess with a world-champion level but are inapt to play  
go. Chess is a purely tactical game while go require a lot of strategy  
(and also tactics for the "life and death" problem, I'm quite sure that  
these are reasonnably easy to solve by computers)
So cald "strategy" video games usually are hard because you start in a vey  
disavantaged position, the computer has more resources than you and can  
click on every units at the same time. hence you need to choose carefully  
your battles, that is crush the computer on the strategy because you can't  
really hope to win purely on tactics.

I've never really like a lot of tactics and usually enjoys more strategy  
and diplomacy in games. Once I've realised that strategy is indeed more  
complicated, I understood why :-)
Of course, that don't mind that tactics is useless or not challenging. I  
mean, computers can trivially solve, say, Sudoku puzzle (they can even  
create them almost instantly) but it's still a lot of fun to play... As  
Doug said, it's funny to try and solve a puzzle (in nethack) but the  
"good" thing to do is to avoid puzzles in the first place. I guess Marvin  
almost never is in a situation were he has to browse twice his inventory  
just to find an item that might help him in his predicament. The key to  
winning is strategy and in such a game that mean don't step in if you  
don't already know out to get out.

